, -5.917881488800049]]}, {"t": " particular", "lp": -0.011879995465278625, "top": [[" particular", -0.011879995465278625], [" specific", -4.487551689147949]]}, {"t": " workload", "lp": -0.09534647315740585, "top": [[" workload", -0.09534647315740585], [" use", -2.4729480743408203]]}, {"t": " that", "lp": -0.33152657747268677, "top": [[" that", -0.33152657747268677], [" where", -1.445479154586792]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.030735917389392853, "top": [["'s", -0.030735917389392853], [" needs", -4.225857734680176]]}, {"t": " prompting", "lp": -0.5891481637954712, "top": [[" prompting", -0.5891481637954712], [" driving", -1.1500078439712524]]}, {"t": " this", "lp": -0.000964391219895333, "top": [[" this", -0.000964391219895333], [" the", -7.247759819030762]]}, {"t": " question", "lp": -0.00023016001796349883, "top": [[" question", -0.00023016001796349883], ["?", -8.433854103088379]]}, {"t": "?", "lp": -0.00019796661217696965, "top": [["?", -0.00019796661217696965], [" (", -9.034345626831055]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.5820778012275696, "top": [["", -0.5820778012275696], [" That", -0.965567409992218]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another engine when your context needs one but none are available\n- More forgiving behavior\u2014workloads are less likely to fail outright\n- May introduce some overhead from the stealing mechanism\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS engines\n- If no CCS is available when needed, the operation may fail or fall back\n- Lower overhead but potentially less robust for workloads that heavily consume CCS resources\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- This is typically the **most restrictive** mode\n- Used when you want explicit control over CCS allocation without any automatic stealing or fallback behavior\n- Useful for **debugging** or when you need deterministic behavior\n\n---\n\n## When to use each\n\n| Mode | Use case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General use, workloads that need CCS but may have variable demand |\n| **2** | When you want predictable resource usage and can ensure CCS availability |\n| **4** | Debugging, testing, or scenarios requiring strict/deterministic CCS behavior |\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm not entirely certain about the exact semantics of mode 4 versus the others, and these driver parameters can vary between driver versions and kernel releases. The Intel Xe driver documentation and changelogs are the authoritative source here.\n\nWould you like me to help you find the specific documentation for your driver version, or are you working on a particular workload that's prompting this question?"}